## Files and links for your research

A resource is a file or a web page you keep with your project: a map of the village, a
photograph, the recording of an interview, an article you found. You attach it to the items it
belongs with, a scene, a chapter or a note. It is not part of your book, so it is never
exported.

The *Resources* panel, on the right of the window, shows the resources of the item you are on.
Open it with its icon, a paper clip, or with Ctrl+Shift+X.

Beside its size, each file shows its extension on a small coloured tag, such as *pdf* or
*docx*. The colour comes from the extension, so files of the same type always share it: a PDF is
red, a Word document blue.

## Adding a resource

- *Add files* chooses one or more files on your computer.
- *Add a web link* asks for an address and, if you like, a title.
- You can also drop files, or a web address from your browser, on the panel. Ctrl+V on the
  list adds the web addresses you copied.

A file is stored in the project: it travels with every copy and every backup of it. A file
larger than 10 MB asks first. *Store it in the project*, or *Link to it instead*: a linked file
stays where it is, and the project only remembers where to find it. *Don't ask again* keeps your
answer. You can change it later in *Settings ▸ Editor ▸ Images & files*, under *Files
over 10 MB*.

When the stored files of a project kept as a single file pass 100 MB, Skribisto offers once to
save it as a folder instead. A folder project saves only what changed.

## Opening a resource

Double-click a resource, or press Enter on it, to open it. Its other actions are in its menu:
the button at the end of its line, or a right click on its tile in a grid.

- A picture opens in the image viewer.
- A web link opens in your browser.
- Any other file opens in the application your system uses for that kind of file.

A file that could start a program, such as a script or an installer, asks first. A project can
come from someone else. *Show in folder* lets you look at it before you decide.

If you change a stored file in another application, a map you annotate or a recording you trim,
Skribisto takes the new version into the project when you come back to its window, before
each save, and when you close the project. A message says so. In a project kept as a single file, its *Undo* puts the previous
version back.

## Putting a resource in your text

Drag a resource from the panel into a text, where you want it.

- A web link becomes a link to its address.
- Any other file becomes a link to the resource. Ctrl+click on it opens the resource. An export
  keeps only its words.
- A picture asks: *Insert the picture* puts the picture itself in the text, and *Insert a link
  to the resource* puts a link.

The menu's *Insert into the text* does the same at the cursor. A resource you drop on a text is
also attached to that text's item.

A file from your computer dropped on a text is added too. A picture goes into the text as a
picture. Any other file becomes a resource of the item, linked where you dropped it. The message
that says so offers *Undo*, which takes back both.

## Attaching to more items

A resource can be on several items at once.

- Drag it onto an item in the binder.
- Or choose *Attach to items…* in its menu, then choose the items in the binder tree.
  Ctrl+click or Shift+click chooses several.

*Detach from this item* takes it off the item you are on; it stays in the project. *Remove from
the project…* takes it out of every item. A message offers *Undo* in both cases.

*Rename and describe…* gives a resource a title and a description of its own.

## A folder's resources

Every folder has a *Resources* view. It shows the resources of the folder and of everything
inside it, each once, with the items it is on.

- *List* shows them one under the other.
- *Grid* shows a tile for each, with a thumbnail for a picture.

The *Gallery* of a notes folder shows picture resources too, marked *Resource*, after the
pictures of the notes' text.

## Resources attached to nothing

A resource is attached to nothing once it is detached from its last item, or once that item is
deleted for good. It stays in the project.

At the top of the *Resources* panel, choose *Unattached* to see them there. It is also a place
for research you have not filed yet:

- what you add while it is chosen, with the buttons, by a drop or with Ctrl+V, is attached to
  nothing;
- *Attach to items…*, in a resource's menu, puts it on items;
- dragging it into a text, or inserting a link to it, attaches it to that text's item.

Choose *This item* to see the resources of the item you are on again.

*Settings ▸ Work ▸ Resources* lists them too. Choose one or several, then:

- *Attach to items…* to put them back on items;
- *Remove from the project…* to delete them;
- *Export copies…* to copy their files to a folder. Their web links are listed in a file named
  links.txt.

The page also says how much room the stored files take.

## When a linked file has moved

A linked file that is not where the project expects it is marked *Not found*. Choose *Locate…*
and show Skribisto where it is now. Any other missing file from the same folder, found in the
new one under its name, is linked again with it.

A linked file is remembered by its place next to the project. Keep the project and its research
side by side, copy both to another computer, and the links still work there.

## Finding a resource

Global search finds resources by their titles, descriptions, file names and web addresses when
its *Resources* switch is on. A replace never changes them.

## From Scrivener

Importing a Scrivener project brings its research files into the new project as resources: the
pictures, PDF files, sounds and web pages of your research folder, attached to the folder they
sat in. Scrivener bookmarks to web pages and to files on your computer become links among the
resources of the document that held them. See [Coming from Scrivener](:help-import-scrivener).
